% man man
     man(1)                     typesetting                     man(1)
     NAME
          man â a page of the manual

     SYNOPSIS
          man [ -v ] [ -m dir ] [ -T pdf ] [ section ] [ title ... ]

     DESCRIPTION
          Man finds the pages of the manual named title and writes
          them for a terminal, as troff(1) does. With a section, a
          number from 1 to 8, only that section's page is written;
          with none, every section's.

          With no title, the section's pages are listed, by groups,
          each with what its NAME says it is; with no section either,
          every section's:

          man 1

          The manual is a tree of files: a page is the file
          dir/section/title. Dir is /sys/man where there is one, and
          /lib/man otherwise.

          -m dir
               The manual is in dir.

          -T pdf
               The page as a PDF file, on the standard output.

          -v   What was not understood in the page is said, on the
               standard error.

          A page is written for man(6)'s macros, or in Markdown's
          lines with a \title{name}{section}{group} as its first line,
          as this one is. The group is where the page is listed:
          shell, files, text, calc, time, processes, system, devtools,
          languages, typesetting, apps, browsers, network, emulators,
          games, in that order, others after them; a page that says
          none, as Plan 9's, is in other. A page of ix's program that
          has a twin in Plan 9 says how the two differ, under
          DIFFERENCES.

     EXAMPLES
          man troff
          man 1
          man 1 man
          man -T pdf troff > /tmp/troff.pdf

     FILES
          /lib/man/?/*

     SEE ALSO
          troff(1)

     DIAGNOSTICS
          A title with no page is said, and the status is no page.
